---
// Timed quiz cards. Each card asks for a number derivable from its stem, checks it against a tolerance, shows the working,
// then asks an intuition follow-up whose reference reasoning is revealed on request. The time targets are this site's
// choice, not any company's. Deterministic: fixed card order, no randomness. Props: title, note.
interface Props {
  title?: string;
  note?: string;
}
const { title = 'Timed cards: answer, then explain why', note = 'time targets are this site\'s choice' } = Astro.props;

const cards = [
  {
    topic: 'ML',
    seconds: 90,
    q: 'One weight is 0.03. Learning rate 0.1, L1 coefficient 0.5, and the data-loss gradient on this weight is 0. After one proximal-gradient (soft-thresholding) step, what is the weight?',
    answer: 0, tol: 0.0005, unit: '',
    working: 'The threshold is learning rate times coefficient: 0.1 × 0.5 = 0.05. Soft-thresholding sets the weight to sign(w) × max(0, |w| − 0.05) = max(0, 0.03 − 0.05) = 0.',
    follow: 'With an L2 penalty (λ/2)w² instead, the same step multiplies the weight by 0.95. Why does L1 land on exactly zero while L2 never does?',
    why: 'The L1 subgradient has magnitude λ whatever the size of the weight, so a small weight is pushed by a fixed amount and the proximal step clips it at zero. The L2 gradient is λw, which shrinks with the weight, so the decay is geometric: 0.03 × 0.95^k is never zero, and falling below 0.001 takes ln(30) / −ln(0.95) ≈ 67 steps. Plain subgradient descent on L1 without the proximal step oscillates around zero instead of landing on it.',
  },
  {
    topic: 'Statistics',
    seconds: 75,
    q: 'An eval of 400 independent items scores 80%. Using the normal approximation, what is the 95% interval half-width, in percentage points?',
    answer: 3.92, tol: 0.2, unit: 'points',
    working: 'Standard error = sqrt(0.8 × 0.2 / 400) = sqrt(0.0004) = 0.02. Half-width = 1.96 × 0.02 = 0.0392, or 3.92 points.',
    follow: 'How many items give a half-width of 2 points, and why does a paired comparison of two models on the same items need fewer?',
    why: 'The half-width scales with 1 / sqrt(n), so halving it from 3.92 to about 2 needs (3.92 / 2)² × 400 ≈ 1,537 items. A paired comparison works on per-item differences; item difficulty that both models share cancels, and only the items where the models disagree carry information, so its variance is usually far below the sum of the two unpaired variances.',
  },
  {
    topic: 'ML',
    seconds: 60,
    q: 'A freshly initialised language model puts uniform probability on a 32,768-token vocabulary. What is the cross-entropy loss per token, in nats?',
    answer: 10.397, tol: 0.05, unit: 'nats',
    working: 'Uniform output gives loss ln(V). 32,768 = 2^15, so ln(32,768) = 15 × ln 2 = 15 × 0.6931 = 10.397.',
    follow: 'The first logged loss is 25. What does that tell you, and what do you check first?',
    why: 'A loss far above ln(V) means the model starts confidently wrong, which a well-scaled initialisation does not do. The usual causes are logits with too large a scale (the output projection initialised or tied without the expected scaling) or a loss reduced by sum where the logging assumes a mean. Both are checked in minutes by printing the logit standard deviation and the reduction mode at step 0.',
  },
  {
    topic: 'CS',
    seconds: 90,
    q: 'You deduplicate 2^20 records by a uniformly distributed 32-bit hash. What is the expected number of colliding pairs?',
    answer: 128, tol: 1, unit: 'pairs',
    working: 'Pairs = n(n − 1) / 2 ≈ 2^40 / 2 = 2^39. Each pair collides with probability 2^−32. Expected collisions ≈ 2^39 / 2^32 = 2^7 = 128.',
    follow: 'Why does the count grow with the square of n, and how wide must the hash be for 2^30 records to expect fewer than one collision?',
    why: 'Collisions are a property of pairs, and the number of pairs grows as n². With 2^30 records there are about 2^59 pairs, so the hash needs more than 59 bits; a 64-bit hash gives 2^59 / 2^64 = 1/32 expected colliding pairs. Silent dedup on a 32-bit hash at this scale drops about 128 distinct records out of 2^20.',
  },
  {
    topic: 'ML',
    seconds: 90,
    q: 'Logits are [2, 1, 0] and the target is class 0. With softmax cross-entropy, what is the gradient of the loss with respect to the first logit?',
    answer: -0.335, tol: 0.005, unit: '',
    working: 'The gradient is p − y. p0 = e² / (e² + e + 1) = 7.389 / 11.107 = 0.665, and y0 = 1, so the gradient is 0.665 − 1 = −0.335.',
    follow: 'Why is every component of this gradient between −1 and 1, and what does that buy over squared error on the probabilities?',
    why: 'The gradient is a difference of two probability vectors, so each component is bounded. Squared error on softmax outputs multiplies the error by the softmax Jacobian, which carries a factor p(1 − p); when the model is confidently wrong the target probability is near 0 and that factor vanishes, so learning stalls exactly where it is most needed. Cross-entropy cancels the saturation.',
  },
  {
    topic: 'Optimisation',
    seconds: 120,
    q: 'Adam with β1 = 0.9 and β2 = 0.999, bias correction switched off by mistake. On the first step, by what factor is the update larger than the bias-corrected update?',
    answer: 3.162, tol: 0.05, unit: '×',
    working: 'After one step m = 0.1g and v = 0.001g². Uncorrected update ∝ m / sqrt(v) = 0.1 / 0.03162 = 3.162 in units of sign(g). Corrected: (m / 0.1) / sqrt(v / 0.001) = 1. Ratio 3.162.',
    follow: 'Does the error shrink after step 1? And why do warm-up schedules exist even with correction on?',
    why: 'The ratio is (1 − β1^t) / sqrt(1 − β2^t). It rises first, because m fills in about ten steps and v in about a thousand: at t = 10 it is 0.651 / 0.0998 ≈ 6.5, and only near t = 1,000 does it fall to about 1.26. Correction removes the bias, not the noise: a second-moment estimate built from ten gradients is unbiased but high variance, and warm-up keeps step sizes small until it settles.',
  },
];
---
<div class="widget ddq" data-ddq={JSON.stringify(cards)}>
  <header><h4>{title}</h4><span class="tiny">{note}</span></header>
  <p class="tiny" data-progress></p>
  <div data-intro>
    <p>Six cards. The timer starts when you open a card; type a number, press Enter or Check, then answer the follow-up in your own words before revealing the reasoning.</p>
  </div>
  <div data-card hidden>
    <p class="ddq-meta"><span data-topic></span> <span role="timer" data-timer></span></p>
    <p class="ddq-q" data-q tabindex="-1"></p>
    <div class="row">
      <label class="tiny" data-ulabel for=""></label>
      <input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="any" style="width: 140px" data-input />
      <button class="btn sm primary" type="button" data-check>Check</button>
    </div>
    <div data-result hidden aria-live="polite">
      <p class="ddq-verdict" data-verdict></p>
      <p data-working></p>
      <p><strong>Follow-up.</strong> <span data-follow></span></p>
      <textarea rows="3" class="ddq-text" aria-label="Your reasoning" data-text></textarea>
      <div class="row"><button class="btn sm" type="button" data-why>Show the reasoning</button></div>
      <p data-whytext hidden></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div data-end hidden><p class="ddq-verdict" data-score tabindex="-1"></p></div>
  <div class="row" style="margin-top: 10px">
    <button class="btn sm primary" type="button" data-next>Open the first card</button>
  </div>
</div>

<style is:global>
  .ddq .ddq-meta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-3); margin: 0 0 6px; }
  .ddq .ddq-q { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 10px; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .ddq .ddq-verdict { font-weight: 650; }
  .ddq .ddq-text { width: 100%; max-width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; font: inherit; font-size: 14px; margin: 6px 0; }
  .ddq .row { flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .ddq [hidden] { display: none !important; }
  .ddq [data-timer] { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
</style>

<script>
  type Card = { topic: string; seconds: number; q: string; answer: number; tol: number; unit: string; working: string; follow: string; why: string };

  const fmt = (s: number) => `${Math.floor(s / 60)}:${String(Math.floor(s % 60)).padStart(2, '0')}`;

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-ddq]').forEach((root, n) => {
    const cards = JSON.parse(root.dataset.ddq ?? '[]') as Card[];
    const $ = <T extends HTMLElement>(sel: string) => root.querySelector<T>(sel)!;
    const intro = $('[data-intro]'), cardBox = $('[data-card]'), result = $('[data-result]'), end = $('[data-end]');
    const input = $<HTMLInputElement>('[data-input]'), check = $<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-check]');
    const next = $<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-next]'), timer = $('[data-timer]'), progress = $('[data-progress]');
    const ulabel = $<HTMLLabelElement>('[data-ulabel]');
    input.id = `ddq-${n}`;
    ulabel.htmlFor = input.id;

    let i = -1, started = 0, tick = 0, correct = 0, onTime = 0, answered = false;

    const stopTimer = () => { if (tick) window.clearInterval(tick); tick = 0; };

    const open = () => {
      i += 1;
      stopTimer();
      intro.hidden = true;
      if (i >= cards.length) {
        cardBox.hidden = true;
        end.hidden = false;
        progress.textContent = '';
        $('[data-score]').textContent = `${correct} of ${cards.length} correct; ${onTime} of those inside the time target.`;
        $('[data-score]').focus();
        next.textContent = 'Run the cards again';
        return;
      }
      const c = cards[i]!;
      end.hidden = true;
      cardBox.hidden = false;
      result.hidden = true;
      $('[data-whytext]').hidden = true;
      $<HTMLTextAreaElement>('[data-text]').value = '';
      answered = false;
      input.value = '';
      input.disabled = false;
      check.disabled = false;
      next.hidden = true;
      progress.textContent = `Card ${i + 1} of ${cards.length}`;
      $('[data-topic]').textContent = c.topic;
      $('[data-q]').textContent = c.q;
      ulabel.textContent = c.unit ? `Answer (${c.unit})` : 'Answer';
      started = performance.now();
      const draw = () => { timer.textContent = `${fmt((performance.now() - started) / 1000)} of ${fmt(c.seconds)}`; };
      draw();
      tick = window.setInterval(draw, 250);
      input.focus();
    };

    const submit = () => {
      if (answered || i < 0 || i >= cards.length) return;
      const raw = input.value.trim();
      if (raw === '' || !Number.isFinite(Number(raw))) { input.focus(); return; }
      answered = true;
      stopTimer();
      const c = cards[i]!;
      const secs = (performance.now() - started) / 1000;
      const ok = Math.abs(Number(raw) - c.answer) <= c.tol;
      const fast = secs <= c.seconds;
      if (ok) correct += 1;
      if (ok && fast) onTime += 1;
      input.disabled = true;
      check.disabled = true;
      $('[data-verdict]').textContent = `${ok ? 'Correct' : 'Not within tolerance'}: the answer is ${c.answer}${c.unit ? ' ' + c.unit : ''}. You took ${fmt(secs)} against a target of ${fmt(c.seconds)}.`;
      $('[data-working]').textContent = c.working;
      $('[data-follow]').textContent = c.follow;
      result.hidden = false;
      next.hidden = false;
      next.textContent = i + 1 < cards.length ? 'Next card' : 'See the score';
      $<HTMLTextAreaElement>('[data-text]').focus();
    };

    check.addEventListener('click', submit);
    input.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => { if (e.key === 'Enter') { e.preventDefault(); submit(); } });
    $('[data-why]').addEventListener('click', () => {
      const w = $('[data-whytext]');
      w.textContent = cards[i]!.why;
      w.hidden = false;
    });
    next.addEventListener('click', () => {
      if (i >= cards.length) { i = -1; correct = 0; onTime = 0; }
      open();
    });
  });
</script>
